Anton Agestam created KAFKA-15869:
-------------------------------------

             Summary: Document semantics of nullable nested API entities
                 Key: KAFKA-15869
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-15869
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Wish
            Reporter: Anton Agestam


The initial version of ConsumerGroupHeartbeatResponse [introduced the first=
 field across the protocol that is a nullable nested entity|https://github.=
com/dajac/kafka/blob/3acd87a3e82e1d2fd4c07218d362e7665b99c547/clients/src/m=
ain/resources/common/message/ConsumerGroupHeartbeatResponse.json#L48]. As t=
he implementor of a third-party schema parser it is not clear how to handle=
 this field, where such fields are allowed, and how null is represented for=
 such fields.

As far as I can tell, the [protocol guide|https://kafka.apache.org/protocol=
.html#The_Messages_ConsumerGroupHeartbeat] does not mention the nullability=
 at all.

The reason I ask where such fields are allowed is because if the answer to =
how null is represented here is just omitting writing any bytes, then I sus=
pect the only unambiguous place for such field to appear would be as the la=
st field of a top-level entity. Even then, how is it discriminated from tag=
ged fields?

Is it possible this field was made nullable by mistake?

=C2=A0



