> The controller is the one that always elects a new leader. When that happe=
ns that metadata is changed on the controller and once committed it=E2=80=99=
s broadcast to all brokers in the cluster. In KRaft this would be via a Part=
itonChange record that each broker will fetch from the controller. In ZK it=E2=
=80=99d be via an RPC from the controller to the broker.
>=20
> In either case each broker might get the notification at a different time.=
 No ordering guarantee among the brokers. But eventually they=E2=80=99ll all=
 know the new leader which means eventually the Produce will fail with NotLe=
ader and the client will refresh its metadata and find out the new one.
>=20
> In between all that leadership movement, there are various ways messages c=
an get duplicated or lost. However if you use the idempotent producer I beli=
eve you actually won=E2=80=99t see dupes or missing messages so if that=E2=80=
=99s an important requirement you could look into that. The producer is desi=
gned to retry in general and when you use the idempotent producer some extra=
 metadata is sent around to dedupe any messages server-side that were sent m=
ultiple times by the client.
